\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\Phi_{E}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\big|_{t}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.7\;{\rm Gy\,h^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.

For a scan the time a given surface element spends in the 0.30 mm
wide beam is the beam dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

where $v_{s}$ is the scan step per unit exposure time.  The absorbed dose
received by a surface element during the passage of the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{p}\simeq\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step per illuminated
point, $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ locally at the focus, $ \approx
3.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm cGy}$ per 20 ms dwell.  With $N_{E}$ energies the
dose scales as $N_{E}D_{p}$; e.g. for the customary two-energy
single-projection the surface dose is $\approx 7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
pixel.  This is the instantaneous, peak surface dose; the time averaged
dose over the whole scanned field is reduced by the duty factor
$w_{f}/L_{{\rm field}}$.

*Remark.*  The calculation is normally quoted as a local dose rate
$\dot D_{t}\approx2\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy/s}$ at the 33 keV focus.  The
$0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell quoted above is the conventional $w_{f}/v_{s}$ dwell
time and gives the $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point figure most often used
in the literature.  Using $W =33.97\,{\rm eV}$ per ion pair the numbers are
$D_{p}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection per energy, essentially the
$\sim$ few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron micro-dose which is quoted for this
class of Laue-monochromator multiple-energy system.

public_text.8:
In an air walled chamber the air kerma, $K$, is related to the ionisation
current $I$ by

\begin{equation*}
K=\frac{I}{e}\frac{W}{m}\qquad \frac{W}{e}=33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

where $m$ is the mass of air which is traversed by the beam.  The air
which is actually traversed at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}
and with an effective chamber length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\,{\rm cm^{3}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m=\rho_{a}V=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\,{\rm
cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}\,{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\,{\rm kg}  .
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m}
      =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}}
             {3.52\times10^{-7}\,{\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho )_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho )_{a}$
the same number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the surface.
The time a given point of the specimen spends in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{\dot s}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure\;time}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose delivered to a point per beam pass, i.e. per
energy step, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}\,{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy at the surface.  In absolute terms

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \Phi E\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big)_{a},
\qquad 
\Phi\approx\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx1.1\times10^{8}
{\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx2.0\times10^{6}\;{\rm ph\,s^{-1}}$ through the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focal spot, consistent with the $I=2.0\;{\rm pA}$ measurement.  

If the $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal extent which is illuminated on the Laue
crystal is scanned with the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot the number
of essentially independent beam positions required to cover the field is

\begin{equation*}
N\approx\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2} ,
\end{equation*}

so that the integrated surface exposure obtained in a complete multiple
–energy raster of the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field is

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm cum}}\approx N D_{1}\approx
1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per surface of the 50 mm field, i.e. $\sim 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ of soft-tissue
absorbed dose for the full multiple-energy scan.  The $\sim 3.9\,\mu{\rm
Gy}$ quoted above is the dose received by any one surface element during
its $0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell in the focal spot, the usual “per-step’’ dose
quoted for synchrotron micro-beam imaging.  This is essentially the
air kerma at 33 keV, $K_{a}\approx\dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$,
and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ it is the
surface absorbed dose in tissue. ∎

public_text.12:
For a parallel plate air chamber the charge which is collected per unit time
is

* $I=e\,{\dot N}_{i}$                   

the number of ion pairs which is formed per unit time is
${\dot N}_{i}=I/e$ and the power which is deposited in the air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}= {\dot N}_{i}W = I\,W
\end{equation*}

with $W\approx 33.97\;{\rm eV}\approx 33.97~{\rm V}$ the $W$ value of air.
Hence

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97~{\rm V}=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm
W}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the air which is actually seen by the beam.  With the
beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and a geometrical path length through the chamber $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ the
mass of air which is seen by the beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1{\rm cm}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the absorbed dose rate in the air which is actually in the beam
is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{I\,W}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\approx
          \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in the air which is in the beam.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same dose rate is
obtained in soft tissue

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \Phi E_{0}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in good agreement with the fluence obtained from

\begin{equation*}
\Phi=\frac{I/e}{A_{f}(E_{0}\mu_{en}L/W)}\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at $E_{0}=33\;{\rm keV}$ and $\mu_{en}=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho=4.24\times10^{-4}\;
{\rm cm^{-1}}$.

During the horizontal scan a point of the object stays in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}
      =0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the $w_{f}/v_{s}$ which is quoted in the statement.  The dose which is
actually received by a surface element which is swept through the beam
in one projection is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of order a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per single energy projection.  In
conventional units

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx 0.003\!-\!0.004\;{\rm mGy}\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell, $\sim 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ while the point
is in the beam.  This is the dose which is normally quoted as the
single–energy surface dose of the micro-beam.  For a $N_{E}$-energy
multiple-energy data set the cumulative surface dose is $N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx 7$–$12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the usual two- to three-energy
dual/triple-energy acquisition, essentially negligible for diagnostic
purposes.  The value quoted above is the familiar $ \approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection, $\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ continuous.

public_text.10:
In air the charge that is collected in an ionisation chamber is

\begin{equation*}
Q=e\,N_{i}=e\,\frac{E_{\rm abs}}{W}
\end{equation*}

so that with the W value of air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm abs}= \frac{W}{e}\,I
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air volume which is illuminated by the
micro beam.  For $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm abs}=33.97\;{\rm V}\times 2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}
      =6.8\times 10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air which is actually in the beam.  At the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times 10^{-6}\ {\rm
m^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with a chamber length $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ in the beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L , \qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}
     =1.293\;{\rm kg\,m^{-3}}\times1.8\times10^{-6}{\rm m^{2}}\times
       0.151{\rm m}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm abs}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
      \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}\approx
      1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm air}$
the same number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus,
$\dot D_{0}\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$.
The photon fluence rate which follows from
$\dot K=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}= \frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx
1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm ph\,m^{-2}s^{-1}},\qquad 
E=33.0\;{\rm keV},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq2.0\times10^{6}\ {\rm ph\,s^{-1}}$ in the $0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
micro beam, the usual synchrotron micro-focus figure.

A material point which is carried through the stationary micro beam
remains in the beam for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{0}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

and the problem gives $t_{0}=w_{f}/(\Delta x/\Delta t)=0.02\;{\rm s}$.
Hence the dose received by a single point in one energy exposure is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta D_{1}= \dot D_{0}\,t_{0}
          \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
               {\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV exposure at the focus, about
$0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ while the beam is on.  This is the commonly
quoted point dose of a synchrotron Laue micro-beam.  In the usual
three energy set the point dose is $\sim 1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$.

If the dose is summed over the whole Laue field which is covered by the
scan, $W_{M}=50\;{\rm mm}$ on the monochromator, the number of independent
steps needed to cover the field is

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}= \frac{W_{M}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the total time the beam is on the $W_{M}\times h_{f}$ field is
$T_{s}=N_{s}t_{0}\approx3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the energy deposited in the field
per energy is

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm field}}\approx \dot D_{0}T_{s}= \Delta D_{1}\,N_{s}
          \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\times1.67\times10^{2}
          \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.6-0.7\ {\rm mGy}$ per energy, i.e. a few mGy for a practical
multiple-energy acquisition, essentially the $N_{s}\approx167$-fold
integrated version of the $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ point dose quoted above.
This is the conventionally quoted $ \sim10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ micro-
focus dose rate and $ \sim10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$ point dose of the system.
∎

4.text:
The ionisation power which is seen in the air which is actually in the
micro-beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W_{a}/e\qquad W_{a}/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is illuminated.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a chamber length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.  Hence the air
kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W_{a}/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}
\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With 
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same rate is the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

while the point is in the beam.  The photon fluence rate which follows
from $\dot K=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}\simeq\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
      \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim2\times10^{6}{\rm ph\,s^{-1}}$ in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
micro beam at 33 keV.

A material point remains in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/\big(\Delta x/t_{exp}\big)\approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted in the statement.  The dose received by a surface
element during one passage of the micro-beam, i.e. one mono-energetic
exposure, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}=\dot D_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV pass,  $ \dot D_{t}\approx0.19\,
{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ while the point is in the beam.  This is the $ \sim
4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ point dose normally quoted for the synchrotron Laue
micro-beam.

For the conventional dual-energy multiple-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
7.8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per surface element, $\sim 8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$, essentially the $4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
mono-energetic value doubled.  With $N_{E}$ energies $D_{cum}=N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ for the usual three-energy set.

Integrated over the $W_{M}=50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan which is mapped onto
the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}=W_{M}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

the total time the beam is on the $W_{M}\times h_{f}$ field per energy is
$T_{s}=N_{s}t_{d}$ and the field-integrated surface kerma per energy is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}=N_{s}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, $\approx0.65\,{\rm mGy}$ for the complete 50 mm fan, the
$N_{s}$-fold integrated version of the $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-step dose
quoted above.  The $1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ local dose rate
and the $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell are the usual
text-book numbers for the 2.0 pA, 33 keV Laue micro-beam, essentially
identical in air and in soft tissue on the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ assumption. ∎

10.text:
In an air chamber the power which is absorbed in the air which is
actually in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}\approx 2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times
33.97\,{\rm V}\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

for $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ in air.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a chamber length $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The air kerma rate in the micro-beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focal spot.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
same number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the surface,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

$\Phi_{0}\simeq\dot K_{a}/E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}\approx1.1\times10^{8}
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ being recovered from
$\dot K=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A material point which is translated through the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
spot stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{\rm exp}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted for the scan.  The dose received by a single
surface element during one mono-energetic passage of the micro-beam is
hence

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\;{\rm ms}$
per 33 keV exposure at the focus, the usual $ \sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron
micro-beam figure.  In air-kerma units

$K_{p}\simeq I W/e\,t_{d}/m_{a}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\,$ per 0.02 s
in-beam exposure.

For the conventional dual-energy material-discrimination acquisition the
dose scales linearly with the number of energies,

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7.8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx 8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, $\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,$Gy
for a three-energy set, essentially negligible for diagnostic purposes.
This is the $ \dot D_{0}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$,
$D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy step normally quoted
for the Laue micro-beam.

Remark on the fan integration.  The Laue fan which is focussed from
$W_{M}=50\;{\rm mm}$ on the crystal to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus
contains

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}=W_{M}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

essentially independent beam positions.  Summed over the whole
$50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field the time the beam is on the field is
$T_{s}=N_{s}t_{d}\approx3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the integrated surface kerma
delivered to the field per energy is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{s}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times
3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, the $\sim$mGy level often quoted for a complete multiple-
energy raster of the Laue fan.  The $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s
dwell quoted above is the per-point, per-energy dose, $D_{2E}\approx
7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ being the customary cumulative dual-energy surface dose
per pixel. ∎

public_text.4:
The ionisation power which is seen in the air which is actually in the
micro-beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W_{a}/e\qquad W_{a}/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is illuminated.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a chamber length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.  Hence the air
kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W_{a}/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}
\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With 
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same rate is the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

while the point is in the beam.  The photon fluence rate which follows
from $\dot K=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}\simeq\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
      \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim2\times10^{6}{\rm ph\,s^{-1}}$ in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
micro beam at 33 keV.

A material point remains in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/\big(\Delta x/t_{exp}\big)\approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted in the statement.  The dose received by a surface
element during one passage of the micro-beam, i.e. one mono-energetic
exposure, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}=\dot D_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV pass,  $ \dot D_{t}\approx0.19\,
{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ while the point is in the beam.  This is the $ \sim
4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ point dose normally quoted for the synchrotron Laue
micro-beam.

For the conventional dual-energy multiple-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
7.8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per surface element, $\sim 8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$, essentially the $4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
mono-energetic value doubled.  With $N_{E}$ energies $D_{cum}=N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ for the usual three-energy set.

Integrated over the $W_{M}=50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan which is mapped onto
the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}=W_{M}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

the total time the beam is on the $W_{M}\times h_{f}$ field per energy is
$T_{s}=N_{s}t_{d}$ and the field-integrated surface kerma per energy is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}=N_{s}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, $\approx0.65\,{\rm mGy}$ for the complete 50 mm fan, the
$N_{s}$-fold integrated version of the $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-step dose
quoted above.  The $1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ local dose rate
and the $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell are the usual
text-book numbers for the 2.0 pA, 33 keV Laue micro-beam, essentially
identical in air and in soft tissue on the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ assumption. ∎

public_text.10:
In an air chamber the power which is absorbed in the air which is
actually in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}\approx 2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times
33.97\,{\rm V}\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

for $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ in air.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a chamber length $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The air kerma rate in the micro-beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focal spot.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
same number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the surface,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

$\Phi_{0}\simeq\dot K_{a}/E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}\approx1.1\times10^{8}
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ being recovered from
$\dot K=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$.

A material point which is translated through the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
spot stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{\rm exp}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted for the scan.  The dose received by a single
surface element during one mono-energetic passage of the micro-beam is
hence

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\;{\rm ms}$
per 33 keV exposure at the focus, the usual $ \sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron
micro-beam figure.  In air-kerma units

$K_{p}\simeq I W/e\,t_{d}/m_{a}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\,$ per 0.02 s
in-beam exposure.

For the conventional dual-energy material-discrimination acquisition the
dose scales linearly with the number of energies,

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7.8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx 8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, $\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,$Gy
for a three-energy set, essentially negligible for diagnostic purposes.
This is the $ \dot D_{0}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$,
$D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy step normally quoted
for the Laue micro-beam.

Remark on the fan integration.  The Laue fan which is focussed from
$W_{M}=50\;{\rm mm}$ on the crystal to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus
contains

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}=W_{M}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

essentially independent beam positions.  Summed over the whole
$50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field the time the beam is on the field is
$T_{s}=N_{s}t_{d}\approx3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the integrated surface kerma
delivered to the field per energy is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{s}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times
3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, the $\sim$mGy level often quoted for a complete multiple-
energy raster of the Laue fan.  The $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s
dwell quoted above is the per-point, per-energy dose, $D_{2E}\approx
7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ being the customary cumulative dual-energy surface dose
per pixel. ∎

11.text:
The power which is actually deposited in the air which is in the
micro-beam at any instant is the ionisation power

\begin{equation*}
 \dot E_{a}=I\,W/e
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm
W}
\end{equation*}

in the air volume which is illuminated at the focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
      =0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}
\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air seen by the beam at any instant.  Hence the air
kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}$ micro-beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
     \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
     \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air in the focal spot.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue while a point lies in the
beam.  In fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}\simeq\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
       \approx1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
       \approx2.0\times10^{6}\;{\rm ph\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times6~{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot, the usual synchrotron
micro-focus value.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30~{\rm mm}$ horizontal spot for
the quoted in-beam time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose received by one surface element on one
mono-energetic passage is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $33\,{\rm keV}$ projection, i.e. $K_{p}=IW/e\,t_{d}/m_{a}\approx4\,
\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell.  This is the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$
synchrotron micro-beam point dose, $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ while the
point is in the beam.

The dose is linear in the number of independent energies used at the
point.  For a multiple energy Laue acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{cum}=N_{E}D_{1}\approx N_{E}\times3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy} .
\end{equation*}

The conventional material discrimination implementation is $N_{E}=2$,

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx2\times3.92\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
     \approx7.6\!-\!8.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per scanned surface element, $\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ for the
usual three-energy set, i.e. $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy.  This
is the operational cumulative surface dose normally quoted for the
Laue multi-energy micro-beam; $D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}$ is
the per-energy, per-point value and $\dot D_{t}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}
\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ is the instantaneous rate while the point is in the
beam.

Remark on the fan integration.  The Laue fan is $W_{M}=50\,{\rm mm}$
on the crystal and is demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{s}=W_{M}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

essentially independent steps.  The beam-on time for the
$W_{M}\times h_{f}$ field per energy is $T_{s}=N_{s}t_{d}\simeq3.3\,{\rm s}$
and the $N_{s}$-fold integrated surface exposure of the whole
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ fan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{s}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per mono-energetic raster, $\approx1.2\!-\!1.4\,{\rm mGy}$ for a
dual-energy raster, the $N_{s}$-fold integrated version of the
$\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-step point dose.  The $3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
$0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell is the dose to an individual surface element; the
$\sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ value is the fan-integrated cumulative exposure of
the complete $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ multiple-energy scan.

